{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"famous-digital-dingo","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Discovered from\n\nStep-7 parity sweep during rebar-debug on bug `itchy-arbored-peccary` (3907-0110-7688-43bd).\n\n## The rule, stated without naming the site\n\n*Every read that hits a snapshot-store entry must record the hit as recency, by bumping the\nentry's `mtime`.* This is the janitor's only victim-selection signal.\n\nADR 0005 D4 states it: \"The janitor evicts LRU by `mtime`, **which the cache bumps explicitly\non every hit**. `atime` is unreliable ..., so it is never used as the recency signal.\"\n`cache.touch_entry` (`src/rebar/_snapshot/cache.py:77-85`) is the mechanism.\n\n## Channel census\n\n| surface | rule holds? |\n|---|---|\n| code entries via `cache.acquire` | ✔ `cache.py:186`, `:192`, `:196` — `touch_entry(dest)` on all three cache-hit re-checks |\n| ticket entries via `materialize_tickets` | **MISSING** — `repo_snapshot.py:676-679`, the `if dest.is_dir(): return str(dest)` cache-hit branch returns without touching |\n| eviction (`_evict`) | ✔ n/a — consumes `mtime`, does not produce it |\n| `startup_sweep` | ✔ n/a — reconciles bytes, not recency |\n| CLI / MCP | ✔ n/a — both reach the store only through the two paths above |\n\n## Consequence\n\nA `tickets-<sha>` entry's `mtime` is frozen at its creation time no matter how many gate runs\nread it. To the janitor an actively-used ticket store looks maximally cold, so it sorts FIRST\nin the LRU order and is the preferred eviction victim — and these are the largest entries in\nthe store (~861 MiB each measured in the wild). Eviction itself stays safe (POSIX\ndelete-on-last-close protects in-flight readers), so this is wasted re-materialization, not\ncorruption.\n\n## Why it was not fixed in 3907\n\n3907 adds the `max_bytes` store-size cap, which makes size-driven eviction possible for the\nfirst time — so it is what makes this gap consequential. But the cap ships **default-off**, so\n3907 introduces no live exposure, and recency is a different rule from 3907's two acceptance\ncriteria (byte accounting and the cap trigger). Filed rather than absorbed.\n\nNote the adjacency to `smart-disloyal-naga` (8386-a512-4815-4e6b), which covers reuse and\nper-review release of these same entries — an un-recorded hit is arguably a reuse that was not\nrecorded. Whoever picks this up should check for overlap first.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `materialize_tickets` bumps the entry's `mtime` on a cache hit, matching `cache.acquire`.\n- [ ] A test observes the frozen `mtime` RED (repeated `materialize_tickets` hits leave `mtime`\n      unchanged) and GREEN after.\n- [ ] A test pins the consequence: a repeatedly-read `tickets-` entry is NOT the first LRU\n      victim ahead of a genuinely older, unread entry.","id":"797c-a4ea-0a64-447a","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"materialize_tickets does not bump mtime on a cache hit, so hot ticket entries look LRU-cold"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787266717214477001,"uuid":"684d1192-be87-460e-970c-920796555869"}